# Next.js security

- Never put secrets in `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`. Only publish anon/public config (e.g. API base URL).
- Call backend via server actions or Route Handlers when cookies/tokens must stay httpOnly.
- Sanitize/escape any user-generated HTML. Prefer text nodes; avoid `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.
- Do not store access tokens in `localStorage` / `sessionStorage`.
- Validate forms client-side for UX; never rely on it for security.
- Use CSRF protections when using cookie-based auth (SameSite + server checks).
- Keep dependency versions patched; avoid installing packages for trivial one-liners.
- External links: `rel="noopener noreferrer"` on `target="_blank"`.
- Do not expose internal IDs or admin APIs in client bundles without auth gates.
- Registration closed and quiz timers are UX only — the API remains the source of truth.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
localStorage.setItem("token", accessToken);

// ✅ GOOD — httpOnly cookie set by backend, or server-side session
// Browser never reads the raw access token in JS
```
